WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.220
- A few months ago.

00:00:01.220 --> 00:00:04.547
I made a video about a
difficult Turkish exam question,

00:00:04.547 --> 00:00:07.580
and that video seemed to
resonate with a lot of people.

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:09.070
There were comments in Turkish

00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:11.530
that were glad that
people could see the pain

00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:12.680
that they had to go through,

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:15.830
and there were plenty of
comments lamenting the state

00:00:15.830 --> 00:00:17.760
of the education system.

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:19.540
Let me show you some of the comments

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:21.340
that gained a lot of support.

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:25.020
This one translates to
you only have a black pen

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:27.060
but create a rainbow.

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:30.300
This is a summary of the
Turkish education system.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:33.720
And this one says that the
questions are of high quality,

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:35.950
but unfortunately, there is no system

00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:39.000
that will take us to the
answers of those questions.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:40.750
Now, I have never studied in Turkey

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:43.350
and I don't know enough
to agree or disagree

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:44.710
with these statements

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:47.200
but they do relate to a larger sentiment

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:49.740
that we see in many other countries.

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:52.680
And that is that the exam
is not the challenge,

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:56.357
the real challenge is competing
against the other students.

00:00:56.357 --> 00:00:59.560
The exam in question is the AYT paper

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:02.830
used to gain entrance
into Turkish universities.

00:01:02.830 --> 00:01:05.970
The population of Turkey is 85 million

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:09.610
and over two million students
are taking this test,

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:12.610
hoping to be in the top 50,000

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:15.060
to get into the good universities.

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:16.920
When entry is so competitive,

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:18.650
you see exams getting harder

00:01:18.650 --> 00:01:20.610
to try and separate people.

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:22.650
But many are left feeling frustrated

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:25.440
by the fact that making exams harder

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:28.080
doesn't raise the quality of education

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:31.580
and instead can leave
students feeling dejected

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:32.920
and hating school.

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:36.130
This comment said geography is destiny.

00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:38.800
And this one said may God help Turkish,

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:41.170
Indian and South Korean students,

00:01:41.170 --> 00:01:44.790
assumably talking about the
tough exams they all have.

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:45.850
Another common thread

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:48.640
was people saying that
even if you're lucky enough

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:51.680
to ace this exam, get
into a good university

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:54.890
and graduate, that there are
still not many jobs waiting

00:01:54.890 --> 00:01:56.300
for you at the end.

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:59.600
This OECD unemployment rate data does show

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:02.750
that Turkey has a pretty
high unemployment rate.

00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:06.190
The latest figures are around 11.2%

00:02:06.190 --> 00:02:09.240
whilst the USA and the UK are at 4%

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:11.890
and Australia, 4.2%.

00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:15.150
It also seems like a lot
of the top students aspire

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:16.780
to go overseas,

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:19.540
contributing to what they call brain drain

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:21.940
where a country's brightest people

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:24.290
aren't sticking around to help it grow.

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:25.990
I planned to work through another one

00:02:25.990 --> 00:02:29.470
of the tough questions
from the AYT exam paper

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:32.100
and I will get to doing that in this video

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:34.810
but first, I got distracted
by another comment

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:38.410
that told me to check out the LGS exam

00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:40.820
given to middle schoolers in Turkey

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:43.270
as a high school entrance exam.

00:02:43.270 --> 00:02:44.620
It is very hard.

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:46.010
A math question this year

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:48.540
was taken from the university exam.

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:50.440
They are literally forcing students

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:55.100
to solve university level
questions at age 13 to 14.

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:57.044
So naturally, I wanted to take a look.

00:02:57.044 --> 00:03:00.480
Here's the paper and
yes, it's really hard.

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:02.560
Apparently, students studying for this

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:05.890
are putting in eight plus hour study days,

00:03:05.890 --> 00:03:08.060
making it the youngest example

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:11.520
of that kind of hard work
I've seen for an exam yet.

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:13.620
Let's take a look at the last question

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:14.930
on the math section.

00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:16.540
We've got these pie charts,

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:19.680
which show the distribution
of televisions sold

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:23.680
in an electronics store in 2019 and 2020.

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:27.310
K, L and M are three different
types of televisions.

00:03:27.310 --> 00:03:31.350
It says that while the number
of L brand televisions sold

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:35.880
in this store in 2020 decreased by 25

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:40.210
compared to 2019, the M
brand televisions increased

00:03:40.210 --> 00:03:41.960
in number by 40.

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:42.992
According to this,

00:03:42.992 --> 00:03:47.992
what is the number of K brand
televisions sold in 2019?

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:51.120
So we wanna know how many televisions

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:53.460
does this slice of pie represent?

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:54.960
That would be easy to do

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:57.770
if, for example, we knew
how many televisions

00:03:57.770 --> 00:03:59.230
were sold in total,

00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:01.780
either in 2019 or 2020.

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:04.070
But that's not information that we have.

00:04:04.070 --> 00:04:05.860
So this becomes quite tricky.

00:04:05.860 --> 00:04:07.840
Please do have a go at solving it

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:08.830
and while you're at it,

00:04:08.830 --> 00:04:10.950
I would like to quickly thank the sponsor

